            "target_entity_instance": "Pre-mixed k-coefficients",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "This technique has been used in 1D atmosphere models (e.g. Marley & Robinson 2015) and the SPARC/MITgcm (Showman et al. 2009). It is not particularly flexible, however, as the local mixing ratios \u03b6i(P, T) must be determined before the time consuming calculation of k-coefficients.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ATMO",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Random overlap with resorting and rebinning",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "In our 1D atmosphere code ATMO we use RORR, usually with about 30 rebinned k-terms in each band. This gives us the flexibility to manipulate gas mixing ratios without recomputing or having to add additional dimensions to the k-table.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ATMO",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "uniform weights",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "For simplicity and ease of implementation we use a much simpler approach for determining the weights w_xy,l^bin: in SOCRATES we use weights given by a Gauss-Legendre quadrature, while in ATMO we use uniform weights, both of which can have an arbitrary number of rebinned k-coefficients n_k^bin.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
